<p>Colombians voted on March 8 to elect a new Congress, while also taking part in coalition-run, primary-style contests to narrow the presidential field ahead of the election in May. Early counts pointed to a fragmented result. President Gustavo Petro’s allied leftist coalition, the Historic Pact, and the main opposition force linked to former President Álvaro Uribe, the Democratic Center, emerged as top Senate vote-getters. But neither looked on track for a majority, keeping coalition-building central to governing after May. Several outlets also flagged low turnout, a reminder of how hard candidates may need to work to bring voters out as the race enters its final stretch. Beyond domestic politics, the vote played out amid reported tensions with the United States, adding an international backdrop to a campaign season in which Colombia’s next leaders will likely have to balance legislative compromise at home with relationships abroad.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/I6X5gPQQyft0I-1yBVkXg7gUTIem2gzG_Js-1pWmJzR2EUsQk7aLXalmYZXDQB78LSaqcfkqXv9eBpglqVHwJdgosnVh-hhCX-EDjUn8BMji9vrCKxX2YSix3BVdk6CBEc202tBAegqbzAw0jqiD1BQjjibvAh2UksJKTE7UFlX0RRs7k1AJbPQDT1dUAFx6--9-Ii4VIgiViKD5JtXEpWQfIh8' alt='Voters participate in Colombia’s legislative election and coalition primaries.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Seat totals: Voters elected 103 Senate members and 183 members of the Chamber of Representatives, according to AP’s overview of the contest.</li><li>Coalition consultations: Alongside the congressional vote, Colombians joined primary-style contests organized by three major coalitions to narrow presidential candidates ahead of May.</li><li>Ballot scale: More than 3,000 candidates ran for Congress, underscoring the breadth of political competition.</li><li>Senate leaders (pre-count): Local media figures cited by MercoPress said Senate bulletin 25 showed Historic Pact with 3,599,411 votes and Democratic Center with 2,473,529.</li><li>Results access: Colombia’s electoral authority, the Registraduría, published official real-time results on its election portal, according to MercoPress.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>MercoPress / local-media pre-count framing: Early bulletins cited by local media put the Historic Pact and the Democratic Center as the two main Senate forces. The vote totals offered an early snapshot as official results kept updating through the Registraduría portal. (<a href='https://en.mercopress.com/2026/03/09/historic-pact-and-democratic-center-lead-colombia-s-senate-race-after-legislative-vote'>MercoPress</a>)</li><li>Analysts cited by The Straits Times: Commentators described the outcome as a divided Congress that will push the next president to build coalitions to pass legislation, rather than govern with a clear majority. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/colombia-elects-new-divided-congress-ahead-of-may-presidential-vote'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>Al Jazeera framing: The election was framed as more than a domestic political test, taking place amid reported US-Colombia tensions and giving the campaign an added foreign-policy backdrop. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/3/8/colombia-elects-congress-chooses-presidential-candidates-amid-us-tensions'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/3/8/colombia-elects-congress-chooses-presidential-candidates-amid-us-tensions'>Colombia elects Congress, chooses presidential candidates amid US tensions</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/colombians-electing-new-congress-choosing-presidential-candidates-130871614'>Colombians are electing a new Congress and choosing presidential candidates</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/colombians-headed-to-the-polls-to-elect-a-new-congress-and-choose-presidential-candidates'>Colombians headed to the polls to elect a new Congress and choose presidential candidates</a> -